        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The limit as $h\\to0$ is missing.",
          "worked": "False. First principles gives $f'(x)=\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-cc-1-3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)=f'(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Both expressions denote the derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-cc-1-3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{y}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is the derivative, whereas $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a ratio of values and is not generally equal to the derivative.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
